# Executive Summary

The Executive Summary answers the question "how did I do in this period?". It
puts your most important numbers on one page, each with its target.

<Video id="executive-summary" />

## What it shows

### The key figures

The strip at the top shows six figures for the period, with the target under
each one:

| Figure | What it measures | Target on the page |
|--------|------------------|--------------------|
| **Time in range** | Time from 70 to 180 mg/dL | 70% or more |
| **Below range** | Time below 70 mg/dL | Less than 4% |
| **Above range** | Time above 180 mg/dL | Less than 25% |
| **eHbA1c** | Your estimated HbA1c, from your average glucose | Below the target under the figure |
| **CV** | Coefficient of variation: how much your glucose goes up and down | 33% or less |
| **Average** | Your average glucose | None |

### Time in Range

The bar shows how your time divides across five ranges: very low, low, in
range, high and very high. Move the pointer over the bar to see the percentage
for each range. If the bar shows a narrower inset inside the in-range part,
that is the tight range, 70 to 140 mg/dL.

**Time Breakdown (per day avg)** turns the percentages into time on an average
day: how long you were in range, low and high.

### eHbA1c and Glucose Stability

- **eHbA1c** estimates what your lab HbA1c would be, from your average glucose.
  Your care team sets your own target. **Clinical details** shows the formula.
- **Glucose Stability** shows the **Standard deviation** and **MAGE** (mean
  amplitude of glycemic excursions). Lower values mean steadier glucose.

### Low and high events

- **Low Blood Sugar Events** splits your time below 70 mg/dL into below
  54 mg/dL and 54-70 mg/dL. **Low episodes** gives the number of separate lows.
- **High Blood Sugar Events** splits your time above 180 mg/dL into
  180-250 mg/dL and above 250 mg/dL.

### What Your Data is Telling Us

This card shows up to three findings from your data, such as strengths or
areas to look at. Some findings have a short clinical note that you can expand.

### Glucose Statistics and Data Quality

- **Glucose Statistics** gives the average, median, lowest and highest glucose.
  Under **Glucose Distribution**, it gives the 10th, 25th, 75th and 90th
  percentiles.
- **Data Quality** shows **CGM Active Time**: the part of the period that your
  CGM sent readings. The target is at least 70% over 14 days. The figures on the
  page are most reliable at 90% or more.

The app shows glucose values in mmol/L if you chose mmol/L.

## How to use it

To see the summary for a longer period:

1. At the top of the report, select the date range.
2. Select **30 Days**.
3. Select **Apply Filters**. All the figures on the page now cover 30 days.

Use the buttons at the bottom of the page to open the
[AGP](/docs/reports/agp), the [Readings](/docs/reports/readings) or the
[Treatment Log](/docs/reports/treatments). To prepare for a clinic visit,
select **Print**.

## Good to know

- If the period has too few days with readings, a badge in the **eHbA1c**
  card shows how many days the estimate uses. See
  [Good to know](/docs/reports#good-to-know) on the reports overview.
- If your CGM active time is below 70%, the **Data Quality** card shows the
  warning "Limited data may affect report accuracy."
